class dvz
{

    /*
    Base case in which we test the checker to see if it works correctly on two basic division operations
    the variable z should not be subject to a warning since we are dividing a number by another different than zero
    the checker should issue a warning for the definition of the variable r since we are trying to divide by zero
    */
    f0()
    {
        def x = 0;
        def y = 7;
        def z = x/y;

        y = 0;
        def r = x/y;

        return;
    }

    /*
    In this case the checker should issue a warning like "This may be possible division by zero" since 
    the condition that set the denominator to zero is inside an if statement
    */
    f1()
    {
        def t = 3;

        if (t > 0)
        {
            t = 0;
        }
        else
        {
            t = 9;
        }

        def v = 7;
        v = v/t;

        return;
    }

    /*
    In this case the checker should issue a warning like "This may be possible division by zero" since 
    the condition that set the denominator to zero is after a while cycle so it's possible to assure the presence 
    of a division by zero only after all the iterations of the cycle
    */
    f2()
    {
        def x = 3;
        def y = 1;

        while (x > 0)
        {
            y = y/x;
            x = x-1;
        }

        def z = y/x;
        
        return;
    }

    */
    This case is pretty similar to the previous one but in this case the divison by zero is performed within the cycle
    */
    f3()
    {
        def x = 3;
        def y = 1;

        while (x > 0)
        {
            x = x/y;
            y = y-1;
        }

        return;
    }
}